-- title:
--   libsodium poly1305_donna.c (donna64) compiled for AArch64, as a placed library
-- statement:
--   The object code of libsodium's portable Poly1305, `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/donna/poly1305_donna.c` at the 1.0.22-RELEASE tag (commit `77e1ce5d`), as a placed library in the `Arm64` semantics (google-deepmind/code-verification's `Arm64.Trusted`, republished as the `Def_Arm64_*` bundles). On AArch64 libsodium compiles this file with 128-bit integers (`HAVE_TI_MODE`), so it includes `poly1305_donna64.h`: Andrew Moon's “poly1305-donna” with three limbs of 44, 44 and 42 bits and 64 × 64 → 128-bit products. libsodium's other Poly1305 implementation uses SSE2 and is built for x86 only.
--
--   **The build.** `poly1305_donna.c` was compiled with zig cc 0.16.0 for `aarch64-linux-musl` at `-O2`, using the flags of DeepMind's `rust-obj2lean print-cflags`, with `-DCONFIGURED -DHAVE_TI_MODE -DNATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN` (what libsodium's configure script defines for this file on a 64-bit little-endian target with 128-bit integers), plus `-mllvm -disable-loop-idiom-all`. Without that last flag the compiler may turn the byte loops of `poly1305_update` and `poly1305_finish` into calls to the C library's `memset` and `memcpy`, which are external functions; with it every loop stays in the object. The object file's SHA-256 is `5cec6fb6…61cf`. The build is reproducible: in a checkout of that commit, `zig cc -target aarch64-linux-musl -mcpu=generic -O2 -g0 -fno-pic -fno-pie -fno-stack-protector -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables -fno-vectorize -fno-slp-vectorize -fno-jump-tables -fno-common -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -fno-ident -mllvm -disable-loop-idiom-all -DCONFIGURED=1 -DHAVE_TI_MODE=1 -DNATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN=1 -Isrc/libsodium/include/sodium -c src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/donna/poly1305_donna.c` gives it. `rust-obj2lean export` turned it into this module. Its byte sidecar is inlined as a literal list, its imports are renamed to the published bundles, and its docstring's account of the upstream build is replaced (the file says so).
--
--   **The library `libsodiumPoly1305Lib`.**
--   - `libsodiumPoly1305Text` is the code block: the 1,396 bytes (349 instructions) of `libsodiumPoly1305PlacedCode`, seven function symbols with their offsets (`crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna` at `0x0`, `_verify` at `0xdc`, `_init` at `0x1c0`, `_update` at `0x20c`, `_final` at `0x2f4`, `poly1305_finish` at `0x30c`, `poly1305_blocks` at `0x43c`; each name carries the suffix “ [libsodium_poly1305_donna]”), and ten relocations the linker resolves: eight calls between these functions, the call of `crypto_verify_16` from `_verify`, and the tail call (a branch) of `sodium_memzero` at the end of `poly1305_finish`.
--   - `crypto_verify_16` and `sodium_memzero` are external symbols, defined in other files of libsodium.
--   - There are no data objects (`libsodiumPoly1305Data` is empty); the function-pointer table `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_implementation` of the source file is not referenced by the code and is not modelled.
--   - There are no exports: all seven functions are `static` in the source. Five of them are reached through that table, and `poly1305_finish` and `poly1305_blocks` by direct calls from the others.
--
--   The statements quantify over every placement of this library that the `Arm64` model accepts (`Obj.PlacedLib.Placeable`), so no load address is fixed.
